  "summary": "A federal jury awarded Alek Schott $76 after finding Bexar County deputies conducted an unconstitutional traffic stop triggered by an ALPR tip from a federal fusion center contact flagging his truck's 'one-day turnaround' as suspicious. The Institute for Justice, which backed the suit, plans to seek an injunction against Bexar County's use of pretextual stops for warrantless searches.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "This is what mass surveillance looks like when it reaches the shoulder of the highway. An AI-powered camera flagged Alek's plate. An anonymous law enforcement agent decided his completely ordinary trip looked suspicious. And a deputy went out and found a violation that never happened.",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    },
    {
      "quote": "Kiki apparently had near real-time access to automated license plate reader data. According to Babb's deposition, Kiki told him that Schott's truck had made a potentially suspicious \"one-day turnaround.\"",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    }
  ],
